              "Layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EIn this demo, Umbraco CTO Filip Bech-Larsen showcases Umbraco Compose, a data orchestration platform built to power modern composable architecture.\n\nYou\u2019ll see how Compose connects multiple data sources \u2014 including Umbraco CMS, Google Sheets, Algolia search, and Cloudflare \u2014 into a single GraphQL layer designed for scalability, performance, and maintainability. Instead of building custom integrations for every client, Compose centralizes and shapes data for delivery across websites, apps, dashboards, and other digital channels.\n\nFilip demonstrates how to ingest 10,000 products in seconds, achieve a 99 Lighthouse performance score, switch data sources without breaking the frontend, and unlock new search capabilities simply by updating a GraphQL query \u2014 no backend rewrites required.\n\nIf you\u0026#39;re building composable digital platforms and want fewer integrations, less maintenance, and faster iteration, this demo shows what composable done right looks like.\n\nChapters:\n00:00 The Problem: Rebuilding custom integrations for every customer\n00:24 What Umbraco Compose Is: Data orchestration \u002B GraphQL at scale\n02:21 Composable Architecture Demo: CMS, Google Sheets, Algolia, Cloudflare\n04:54 High-Volume Ingestion \u002B Performance (10,000 products, Lighthouse 99)\n06:29 Switching Data Sources \u002B Enhancing Search with GraphQL\n\nDiscover more about Umbraco Compose: https://umbraco.com/products/umbraco-compose/\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VnxqTnMjSYE\u0022 target=\u0022_blank\u0022 rel=\u0022noopener\u0022\u003ERead the original article\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
